Q: How to split long-single-line-strings into virtual lines while ensuring that the final result is still a single-long-line at runtime? A: <h1>Splitting Long Single-Line Strings in C#</h1>
<p>Here are the practical approaches, ranked by cleanliness:</p>
<h2>1. String Concatenation with <code>+</code> (Best Option)</h2>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>var message =
    &quot;Lorem ipsum dolor sit amet, consectetur adipiscing elit. Donec a diam lectus.\n&quot; +
    &quot;\n&quot; +
    &quot;Sed sit amet ipsum mauris. Sed sit amet ipsum mauris. Sed sit amet ipsum mauris. &quot; +
    &quot;Sed sit amet ipsum mauris. Maecenas congue ligula ac quam viverra nec consectetur &quot; +
    $&quot;ante hendrerit. Donec et mollis dolor: {customMessage}&quot;;
</code></pre>
<p><strong>Addressing the OP's concern about performance:</strong> The C# compiler <strong>concatenates constant string literals at compile time</strong>. <code>&quot;Hello &quot; + &quot;World&quot;</code> compiles to the single string <code>&quot;Hello World&quot;</code> — no runtime allocation. When interpolation/variables are involved, the compiler emits an optimized <code>string.Concat</code> or <code>DefaultInterpolatedStringHandler</code> call, which is already the most efficient single-allocation path. There is <strong>no cascading intermediate string creation</strong>.</p>
<p>This is genuinely the idiomatic C# solution.</p>
<h2>2. The Raw String <code>{&quot;&quot;}</code> Trick (OP's Own Answer)</h2>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>var message = $&quot;&quot;&quot;
               Lorem ipsum dolor sit amet, consectetur adipiscing elit.{&quot;&quot;
               }Donec a diam lectus.

               Sed sit amet ipsum mauris. Sed sit amet ipsum mauris.{&quot;&quot;
               }Sed sit amet ipsum mauris. Sed sit amet ipsum mauris.{&quot;&quot;
               }Maecenas congue ligula ac quam viverra nec consectetur{&quot;&quot;
               }ante hendrerit. Donec et mollis dolor: {customMessage}
               &quot;&quot;&quot;;
</code></pre>
<p>Clever, but it's a hack that hurts readability. The <code>{&quot;&quot;}</code> interpolation hole evaluates to an empty string, and by placing the closing <code>}</code> on the next line, the newline becomes part of the interpolation whitespace (not the string content). It works but is obscure.</p>
<h2>3. A Helper Method (If This Pattern Recurs)</h2>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>public static string JoinLines(params string[] parts) =&gt; string.Join(&quot;&quot;, parts);

var message = JoinLines(
    &quot;Lorem ipsum dolor sit amet, consectetur adipiscing elit. Donec a diam lectus.\n&quot;,
    &quot;\n&quot;,
    &quot;Sed sit amet ipsum mauris. Sed sit amet ipsum mauris. &quot;,
    &quot;Sed sit amet ipsum mauris. Sed sit amet ipsum mauris. &quot;,
    &quot;Maecenas congue ligula ac quam viverra nec consectetur &quot;,
    $&quot;ante hendrerit. Donec et mollis dolor: {customMessage}&quot;
);
</code></pre>
<h2>Verdict</h2>
<p><strong>Use <code>+</code> concatenation.</strong> The performance concern raised by the OP is a myth for this scenario — the compiler and runtime handle it optimally. It's the most readable, most portable, and most idiomatic approach. No language feature like <code>\</code> line continuation is needed when the existing tooling already does the right thing.</p>
<p>The broader discussion about resources/XAML dictionaries is valid for localization-heavy apps but is orthogonal to the formatting question itself.</p>
